re difficult  not because john sellers has made it  difficult for you so that's that's an  excellent thing to be able to say  um i think  that another really good point about  this work i mean to begin with  the scholarship is just  superb  you know  it's it's heavily  footnoted which i'm going to talk about  a little bit later there's all these  great discussions everything is being  referenced and explained  and you know john is one of these people  who has read and not just read but  studied  very very wild widely and not just in  ancient philosophy  he understands ancient philosophy  through  you know contemporary people today  talking about it but also the grand  sweep of history as well  so that's really quite admirable he's  attentive to the differences not just  between  philosophical schools like the  aristotelians versus the platonist or  the stoics versus the skeptics but also  within  a school itself like the stoics so you  know where for example  um  one stoic thinks one thing and another  thinks another thing you can count on  john to actually bring that to light and  to say okay these are you know differing  conceptions here let's not just blur  over these let's pay attention to what's  happening there another thing that i  think is really really great about this  book  is  following out these other distinctions  like you know his discussion of the  different kinds of arts and so thinking  very  seriously about what is what is this art  of living that we're calling philosophy  if we're looking at it in a stoic  perspective or we're looking at it in  other perspectives as well that's a  really solid  piece of not just scholarship but also  like deep thinking about these these  matters that is guided by the the you  know thinkers who he's engaging with um  the meaning of esthesis  that's something that is being explored  here as well a lot of people like to use  that word because it's a cool greek term  what does it actually mean you know it's  not meaning exactly the same thing  to plato as it is to the stoics and you  know being able to explain that i think  is is quite good  um  examining counter positions you know  thinking about aristotle's take on these  things or  thinking about the skeptics that entire  chapter  considering mostly sextus empiricus and  his views but you know  situating it within a a paradigm  provided by  skeptical philosophy in antiquity which  took over plato's academy by the way and  sextus you know is  uh you know responding later to both to  pyronism  and to academic skepticism so that's a  really great strength of the work i  think  i also find  that you know in chapter seven  rereading this  um  i actually you know i'm i'm pretty much  convinced by john seller's  reinterpretation  of the meditations in light of the three  disciplines  and i think it's it's it's a good  interpretation john sellers isn't the  only person obviously to bring this up  you know pure dough does as well but i  think what's in here is really really  quite a good  interpretation so it's plausible but  man it also provides you with sort of  like a model of what that kind of  interpretation ought to look like so  that's pretty cool as well  so you know a lot of very positive  things to say about this book  it's the kind of book that as i've said  before with a few other  reviews that i've done  as a researcher as a writer  you read a book like this and you're  like oh that is so good i wish i was  able to write a book like that myself so  there's a bit of not envy but emulation  involved as well in in reading this but  i think for  most other readers i mean they might  have that reaction but they're also  going to be like wow this is really  helpful for me  i only have two things that i'm going to  call problematic here and they're not  like deeply problematic they're they're  things where there can be  reasonable disagreement about these  matters so i'm i may be at odds  either with other readers or with with  john sellers on these sorts of things  and the first one is about his  interpretation of how the end caridian  or the handbook of epictetus is  structured in chapter  six you know i you know i think that  epictetus does have three topoi right i  i buy into that completely that  epictetus is originating this um i i  don't see that as as you know central to  stoic philosophy in general and i also  don't think that the three topoi line up  neatly  with um the physical the ethical and the  logical  so you know when sellers is saying that  sections two through twenty nine  the first of the three types of exercise  in the handbook is concerned with  desires and aversions and this can can  be seen to correspond to physics i'm  like  yeah i don't know about that that seems  a little bit too neat and here's here's  one of the reasons why i find  his interpretation here  plausible interesting but problematic  simplicius  who he brings up you know who wrote a  commentary on epictetuses and coridian  he also says well you know epictetus is  in caribbean is structured in these ways  and it you know falls into these  different sections and it doesn't map on  to what john sellers is doing  so you gotta say all right there's  there's  multiple  plausible  constructions organizations of epictetus  and caribbean this one can't be just the  you know automatic intuitive one it's a  plausible one but you know there's  multiple plausible ones so that's one  thing to say right now the other thing  to say  i'm actually i don't see it as  problematic but i can see how some  readers might view things as problematic  i'm going to show you a typical  set of pages  all right so you look at that right  there what are you seeing  well there's a lot of footnote right  and almost every single page of the book  has a lot of footnotes  if you don't like that you're going to  be bothered by that in this book and  you're going to be constantly going oh  i'm missing something in the footnotes  right  if you like footnotes which i do i i  grew up as a reader reading books where  they were extensively footnoted and um  oftentimes there's really cool  discussions in the footnotes i look at  them as sort of like oh these like  little side  conversations if you like that sort of  thing that's not problematic at all to  you and you're going to love it because  again just about every single page in  the main body of the text has got some  some footnotes there's a few pages that  don't have footnotes but they're uh  they're the rarity so those are the only  things that i really find problematic  about this this book  final thoughts if you've made it this  far in the review i think you've figured  out that i think this is a really great  book that if you have any interest in  philosophy  as an art of living if you have an  interest in stoicism if you have an  interest in ancient philosophy in  general you want this book this should  be on your shelf it should be part of  your  library  so you know i highly recommend it  unqualifiedly  recommend it  i would say get this i also want to say  thank you once again to  dominic  escorpessel for sending me this copy of  the book i know it's taken me a little  bit longer to get around to a review as  promised but i have now delivered on  that promise so thanks to him thanks to  john for for writing this excellent book  and  that's it i i highly recommend it  you
 

